var definitions = ['<h2>C’est mon chapeau.</h2><div><strong>C’</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (which means "this" or "it") and "est" (which is the third-person singular present tense form of "être", meaning "to be"). In this case, "C’" functions similarly to "it’s" in English and is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is contained within the contraction "C’est" and is the third-person singular present tense of "être", which means "to be". In this context, it links the subject to the complement "mon chapeau", indicating possession.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "my" in English. It is masculine singular to agree with the noun "chapeau", which is masculine.</div><div><strong>chapeau</strong> This is a French noun that means "hat" in English. It’s the object of the sentence, owned by the speaker.</div>','<h2>Mon père</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French that means "my". It suggests ownership or a relationship to the following noun. It is masculine singular to match the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a French noun meaning "father". It is masculine and singular, and in this context, it is owned or related to the speaker.</div>','<h2>Ma nouvelle voiture</h2><div><strong>Ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for the first-person singular feminine, equivalent to "my" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "voiture" (a feminine noun).</div><div><strong>nouvelle</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "new". It comes before the noun and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, "voiture". Since "voiture" is feminine singular, "nouvelle" also takes the feminine singular form.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "car". It is a feminine singular noun, hence the previous words "ma" and "nouvelle" agree with it in gender and number.</div>','<h2>Mes framboises.</h2><div><strong>Mes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for the first-person singular, equivalent to "my" in English. It indicates possession or belonging, and agrees in number with the noun it modifies, which is plural in this case.</div><div><strong>framboises</strong> This is the French word for "raspberries". It is a feminine plural noun, which is why the possessive adjective "mes" is also in the plural form.</div>','<h2>Ta mère.</h2><div><strong>Ta</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English when speaking about something belonging to someone you address informally. It’s in the feminine singular form to agree with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mother". It is a feminine noun, which is why it is preceded by the feminine singular possessive adjective "ta".</div>','<h2>Ta meilleure amie</h2><div><strong>Ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for "your" when speaking about a feminine singular noun. It denotes possession or association with the person addressed, who is assumed to be familiar with the speaker (informal "you").</div><div><strong>meilleure</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "meilleur", which means "best". In this context, it qualifies the noun "amie" to indicate the highest degree of friendship.</div><div><strong>amie</strong> This is a French noun for "female friend". It is the feminine form of "ami", which denotes a male friend. It’s the subject referred to by the possessive adjective "ta".</div>','<h2>Tu prends ton café devant ton ordinateur.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person informally. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>prends</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "prendre", which means "to take" or "to have" (in the context of consuming food or drinks). It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>ton café</strong> <strong>ton</strong> is a possessive adjective meaning "your" indicating ownership or association to the second-person singular. <strong>café</strong> means "coffee" in English. Together they mean "your coffee", which is the object of the action.</div><div><strong>devant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "in front of". It describes the location or the place where the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>ton ordinateur</strong> Similarly to the above, <strong>ton</strong> is the possessive adjective "your" corresponding to the second-person singular. <strong>ordinateur</strong> means "computer" in English. Together they indicate "your computer", specifying the location in relation to the action.</div>','<h2>Tu joues avec tes jouets.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>joues</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". It indicates the action being done by the subject.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "with". It indicates the relationship between the action of playing and the objects being played with.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the second-person singular possessive adjective in French, meaning "your". It describes ownership of the items by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>jouets</strong> This is the plural form of "jouet", which means "toy" in English. The plural is used here to imply that there are multiple toys involved in the action.</div>','<h2>Ses parents</h2><div><strong>Ses</strong> This is a French possessive adjective meaning "his", "her", or "their". It’s used to indicate possession and matches the gender and number of the noun it modifies. In this case, it’s plural to match "parents".</div><div><strong>parents</strong> This is a French noun meaning "parents". It’s in the plural form to indicate both the mother and father or parental figures.</div>','<h2>Il passe beaucoup de temps dans son kayak.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>passe</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "passer", which generally means "to pass" but in this context translates to "spends". It indicates the action of using time.</div><div><strong>beaucoup</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "a lot" or "much". It describes the extent of time spent.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This preposition is used here in the partitive sense to mean "of"; it links "beaucoup" to "temps" to indicate a portion or quantity of time.</div><div><strong>temps</strong> This is a French noun meaning "time". In combination with "beaucoup de", it expresses the quantity of time spent.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition that translates to "in" in English. It is used to indicate the location where the time is spent, in this case, within the kayak.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his" in English. It indicates that the kayak belongs to the subject "il".</div><div><strong>kayak.</strong> This is a noun borrowed from English and is the same in French. It refers to the small, narrow watercraft that is maneuvered using a double-bladed paddle.</div>','<h2>Sa grande soeur.</h2><div><strong>Sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "his" or "her". It is feminine and singular to agree with the gender and number of the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>grande</strong> This is an adjective meaning "big" or "tall". In this context, it can also mean "older" when referring to siblings. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is feminine singular in this case.</div><div><strong>soeur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "sister". It is feminine and singular to match with the possessive adjective and the adjective describing it.</div>','<h2>Elle s’est endormie sur ses livres.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the French third-person singular pronoun, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>s’est</strong> This is a contraction of "se est", where "se" is a reflexive pronoun and "est" is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être" (to be), used here as an auxiliary verb in forming the reflexive past tense.</div><div><strong>endormie</strong> This is the past participle of the reflexive verb "s’endormir", meaning "to fall asleep". The participle agrees in gender and number with the feminine subject "elle".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "on" in English. It indicates the position or location of the subject.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, meaning "her" in English. It agrees in number with the plural noun "livres", indicating possession.</div><div><strong>livres</strong> This is the French word for "books". It’s a plural noun and is the object of the preposition "sur".</div>','<h2>La mère de Paul; sa mère.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in French for feminine singular nouns, it translates to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mother". It’s a common noun referring to the female parent of a child.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition often translated as "of" in English. It is used here to indicate a relationship or possession, linking "mother" to "Paul".</div><div><strong>Paul</strong> This is a proper noun, a masculine first name in both English and French. In this context, it refers to the person whose mother is being mentioned.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "his" or "her", used here to indicate that the "mother" belongs or is related to the previously mentioned person, Paul. It is the feminine singular form to agree with "mère" which is feminine.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is repeated from earlier in the sentence and means "mother". Since this mention follows the possessive adjective "sa", it is understood to specifically refer to Paul’s mother.</div>','<h2>Le père de Paul; son père.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, translating to "the" in English. It points to a specific noun.</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a French noun meaning "father". In this context, it acts as the main subject of the phrase.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that can have several meanings, including "of" or "from". Here, it’s used to indicate possession, as in "of Paul".</div><div><strong>Paul</strong> This is a proper noun, a personal name. In the phrase "Le père de Paul", it specifies whose father is being referred to.</div><div><strong>;</strong> This semicolon is a punctuation mark used to separate items in a list or clauses.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a French possessive adjective meaning "his" or "her". It relates to the noun "père" indicating possession. It’s gender of the owner does not imply the gender of the subject, but "père" being masculine makes "son" also appear in masculine singular form.</div><div><strong>père</strong> As previously mentioned, this noun means "father". It’s used here to refer to the father once more, but this time indicated with the possessive adjective "son".</div>','<h2>Le père de Marilyne; son père.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article "the" used for masculine singular nouns. It specifies a particular noun that is known to the speaker and listener.</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French meaning "father". It represents a male parent.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition that means "of" or "from". It is used to indicate a relationship or possession between "père" and "Marilyne".</div><div><strong>Marilyne</strong> This is a proper noun, a female given name. In this context, it’s used to specify whose father is being referred to.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "her" or "his" in English. It indicates that something belongs to or is associated with a person. In this context, since "Marilyne" is mentioned previously, "son" implies "her".</div><div><strong>père.</strong> This noun is repeated for emphasis and clarity, referring back to "the father". It is the subject of the second clause, being equated to "le père de Marilyne" from the first clause.</div>','<h2>La mère de Marilyne; sa mère.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in French that translates to "the" in English. It refers to a specific noun that is feminine and singular.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is a noun meaning "mother" in English. It is feminine and singular, matching the article "la".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that translates to "of" in English. It is used here to show possession or relation.</div><div><strong>Marilyne</strong> This is a proper noun, a given name for a female in French. In this context, it is used to specify whose mother is being referred to.</div><div><strong>;</strong> This semicolon is used in writing to connect two related independent clauses or to separate items in a complex list.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective that means "her" in English when referring to a feminine noun. It indicates that the "mother" in the next part of the sentence belongs to a female subject, presumably Marilyne.</div><div><strong>mère.</strong> This is the same noun as before, meaning "mother". In this second clause, the phrase emphasizes the possession "her mother".</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>J’ai enfin obtenu mon diplôme!</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je", used before a vowel or mute h. It is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the compound past.</div><div><strong>enfin</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "finally". It denotes the conclusion of a process or the arrival at a long-awaited point in time.</div><div><strong>obtenu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "obtenir", which means "to obtain". In this sentence, it combines with "ai" to form the past compound tense known as passé composé.</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "my" in English. It agrees with diplôme, which is a masculine noun.</div><div><strong>diplôme</strong> This is a French noun meaning "diploma" or "degree". It refers to a certificate awarded upon the completion of a course of study.</div>','<h2>Mon nouveau petit frère dort.</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "my". It is used here to indicate possession or relationship and agrees with the masculine singular noun that follows.</div><div><strong>nouveau</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "new". Here, it’s used before the noun to describe something recently acquired or arrived. The adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>petit</strong> This is an adjective in French meaning "small" or "little". In this context, it is used to describe someone younger or smaller. As with "nouveau", it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>frère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "brother". It’s in the singular form and is the object of possession in the sentence, being owned or related to the subject.</div><div><strong>dort</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "dormir", which means "to sleep". It indicates the action being performed by the subject in the sentence.</div>','<h2>C’est mon anniversaire aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "Ce" (this/it) and "est" (is), the third-person singular present tense form of "être" (to be). In English, it translates to "It’s" or "This is".</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective for first-person singular in French, meaning "my". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it is describing, which in this case is "anniversaire" (birthday), a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>anniversaire</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French that means "birthday". It indicates the annual celebration of the date on which a person was born.</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is an adverb in French meaning "today". It specifies the time at which the statement is applicable.</div>','<h2>Mon mari est encore en vacances.</h2><div><strong>Mon</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "my". It indicates possession or a relationship of belonging. It’s masculine singular to match the masculine noun it modifies ("mari").</div><div><strong>mari</strong> This is a noun in French that translates to "husband" in English. It refers to a married man.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "mari" with the information that follows - that he is "on vacation".</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "still" or "again" in English. It indicates the continuing or repeating nature of the action or state it describes.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a preposition that, in this context, is part of the expression "en vacances" which means "on vacation" in English.</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a noun meaning "vacation" or "holidays" in English. It’s used here in the plural form which is common in French when talking about taking a break or a period of time off work.</div>','<h2>Le plombier a pu réparer mon évier.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It signifies a specific noun that is known to the speakers.</div><div><strong>plombier</strong> This is a French noun meaning "plumber". It designates the tradesperson specializing in the installation and maintenance of systems used for water, sewage, and drainage in plumbing systems. Here, it’s in the masculine singular form to agree with the article "le".</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it’s used as a helping verb to form the past tense, known as the "passé composé".</div><div><strong>pu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". When combined with "a" from "avoir", it expresses the past ability, in this case, "was able to" or "could".</div><div><strong>réparer</strong> This is the French verb meaning "to repair". It is the main action in the sentence and is in its infinitive form since it’s being used with the auxiliary verb "a pu".</div><div><strong>mon</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French equivalent to "my" in English. It shows ownership or association with the speaker and must agree with the noun it modifies in gender and number. Here it’s masculine singular to modify "évier".</div><div><strong>évier</strong> This is a French noun meaning "sink". It refers to the kitchen fixture that the plumber has repaired. It is masculine singular, matching the article "mon".</div>','<h2>C’est ma journée de congé.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "Cela est" in French, which translates to "It is" or "This is" in English. It’s often used to introduce a situation or state of affairs.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective, feminine singular form of "my" in English. It indicates that the following noun, which is feminine, belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a French noun meaning "day". It is feminine and denotes either the daytime period of 24 hours or a specific day with an activity or event.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that can mean many things depending on context. In this sentence, it means "of" establishing a relationship between "journée" and "congé".</div><div><strong>congé</strong> This is a noun that means "day off" or "leave". It refers to time off from work or duty.</div>','<h2>Je dépendais de ma mère.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>dépendais</strong> This is the first-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "dépendre", which means "to depend". It indicates a past ongoing state or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a French preposition generally meaning "from" or "of". In this context, it is used to indicate dependence on something or someone.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the first-person singular, meaning "my" in English. It is used here to modify "mère" (mother), indicating possession.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mother". It is the object of dependence in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que vous connaissez ma soeur?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a question phrase in French literally translating to "is it that". It is used to indicate that what follows is a question.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. In this context, it can indicate either a formal singular or any plural address.</div><div><strong>connaissez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural of the present tense of the verb "connaître", which means "to know" in the sense of being acquainted with someone or something.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "my". It indicates that something belongs to the speaker and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is feminine singular in this case.</div><div><strong>soeur</strong> This is the French word for "sister". It is a feminine noun matching the feminine possessive adjective "ma".</div>','<h2>Je porte un chapeau sur ma tête.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>porte</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "porter", which means "to wear" or "to carry". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>chapeau</strong> This is a French noun meaning "hat". It is masculine and singular, matching the preceding article "un".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "on" in English. It indicates the location or position of the object.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "my" in English. It indicates that the next noun belongs to the speaker, and it is feminine and singular to match the noun it describes, even when the speaker might be male.</div><div><strong>tête</strong> This is a French noun meaning "head". It is feminine and singular, which is why the possessive adjective "ma" is used instead of "mon", aligning with the gender of the noun instead of the speaker. </div>','<h2>C’est ma première journée de travail.</h2><div><strong>C’est</strong> This is a contraction of "ce" (it/this) and "est" (is) from the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it’s used to indicate "It’s" or "This is" in English.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective "my" in English, agreeing in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is feminine singular.</div><div><strong>première</strong> This is the feminine form of "first" in English and it’s an ordinal number. Again, it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "day" in English. In this context, it refers to a workday or the duration of a day’s work.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that can have several meanings, such as "of" or "from" in English. Here, it’s used to link "journée" to "travail" indicating possession.</div><div><strong>travail</strong> This is a masculine noun that means "work" or "employment" in English.</div>','<h2>Elle chante ma chanson préférée.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>chante</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "chanter", which means "to sing". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "my" in English. It signifies ownership or relation to the speaker, and it has to agree in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is "chanson" in this case.</div><div><strong>chanson</strong> This is a French noun meaning "song". It is feminine singular to match the possessive adjective "ma".</div><div><strong>préférée</strong> This is the past participle of the French verb "préférer", which means "to prefer". Here, it’s used as an adjective to describe the noun "chanson". It’s in the feminine singular form to agree with the noun that it describes.</div>','<h2>Oui, ma température est un peu élevée.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds affirmatively to a question.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective "my" in French, used here to denote possession or association with the speaker, who is female. If the speaker were male, it would still be "ma" because température is feminine in French.</div><div><strong>température</strong> This noun in French means "temperature". It refers to the measure of body heat in this sentence, indicating a fever or higher-than-normal body temperature.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It’s used here to link the subject "ma température" with the adjective "élevée".</div><div><strong>un peu</strong> This phrase means "a little" in English. It’s an adverbial phrase that modifies the adjective "élevée", indicating the degree to which the temperature is elevated.</div><div><strong>élevée</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "élever", which means "to raise" or "to elevate". When used as an adjective, as it is here, it means "elevated" or "high". In agreement with "température", it takes the feminine form. If referring to a masculine noun, it would be "élevé".</div>','<h2>Je parle à ma fille au téléphone.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It is the subject of the sentence who is performing the action.</div><div><strong>parle</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "parler", which means "to speak" or "to talk". Here it indicates the action the subject is performing.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that can mean "to" among other definitions. In this context, it’s used to indicate the indirect object of the verb "parle" —the person who is being spoken to.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is a possessive adjective, meaning "my" in English. It denotes ownership or a relation to the speaker. It’s feminine singular to match the gender and number of the noun it modifies, "fille".</div><div><strong>fille</strong> This is a French noun meaning "daughter". Here, it’s the indirect object of the sentence, the person to whom the speaker is talking.</div><div><strong>au téléphone</strong> This phrase means "on the phone" in English. It’s formed from the preposition "au" (a contraction of "à le", which changes to "au" before a masculine singular noun) and the noun "téléphone".</div>','<h2>Je gagnerais la course si ma jambe n’était pas cassée.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>gagnerais</strong> This is the first-person singular conditional tense form of the verb "gagner", which means "to win". It indicates an action that would happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>la course</strong> This phrase means "the race" in English. "La" is a definite article in the feminine singular form, and "course" is a feminine noun referring to a race or a running competition.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This word translates to "if" in English. It introduces a condition in the sentence.</div><div><strong>ma jambe</strong> This phrase means "my leg" in English. "Ma" is a possessive adjective in the feminine singular form that agrees with the feminine noun "jambe".</div><div><strong>n’était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". Here, it is used in a conditional clause to express a past state that is contrary to fact.</div><div><strong>pas cassée</strong> This is the past participle of "casser", which means "to break", with the negation "pas", to indicate "not broken". It is in the feminine singular form to agree with the noun "jambe".</div>','<h2>Merci! Je perds toujours mes clés.</h2><div><strong>Merci!</strong> This is the French word for "thanks" or "thank you". It expresses gratitude.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>perds</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "perdre", which means "to lose". It indicates the action that the subject is doing.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It describes the frequency with which the action of the verb occurs.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, meaning "my" in English. It shows ownership or relation to the subject "Je" and is plural because it refers to multiple items, "clés" (keys).</div><div><strong>clés</strong> This is the French word for "keys". It is the direct object of the verb "perds" – the thing that is being lost.</div>','<h2>Je peux choisir mes propres vêtements.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the ability of the subject to perform an action.</div><div><strong>choisir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to choose" or "to select" in English. As an infinitive, it acts as the main verb in the sentence describing the action that the subject can perform.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the first-person singular, which translates to "my" in English. It shows ownership or relation to the following noun.</div><div><strong>propres</strong> This is an adjective that means "own" in English. It emphasizes the possession or personal relation to the subsequent noun.</div><div><strong>vêtements</strong> This is a French noun meaning "clothes" or "garments". It’s the direct object of the verb "choisir", referring to the items being chosen.</div>','<h2>Ah oui? Je peux acheter mes billets ici?</h2><div><strong>Ah oui?</strong> This is an expression of surprise or realization in French, similar to "Oh really?" or "Is that so?" in English.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>peux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the speaker’s ability or possibility to do something.</div><div><strong>acheter</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French meaning "to buy". When used with another verb (in this case "peux"), it remains in the infinitive form.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, the possessive form of "my" in English, used here in the plural form to indicate ownership of multiple items by the speaker.</div><div><strong>billets</strong> This is a French noun meaning "tickets". It’s in the plural form to correspond with "mes", indicating that the speaker is referring to multiple tickets.</div><div><strong>ici?</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "here". When placed at the end of a question, it asks if the action can be done in this place.</div>','<h2>Elles refuseront toujours d’être mes amies.</h2><div><strong>Elles</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English when referring to a group of females or a mixed group where the feminine is emphasized. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>refuseront</strong> This is the third-person plural future tense form of the verb "refuser", which means "to refuse". It indicates that the action of refusing will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This word translates to "always" in English. It’s an adverb used to describe the frequency of the action, here implying that the refusal is consistent or permanent.</div><div><strong>d’être</strong> This is the infinitive form of "être" preceded by "de", which collectively means "to be" in English. The infinitive is used here to express what the subjects will refuse to do.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is a possessive adjective in the first-person singular, meaning "my" in English. It indicates possession or association with the speaker.</div><div><strong>amies</strong> This word means "friends" in English, with the feminine plural form matching the feminine subject "Elles". It refers to the people they refuse to become for the speaker.</div>','<h2>Je vais penser à toi pendant mes vacances.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". In this sentence, it is used to form the near future tense, indicating an action that will happen soon.</div><div><strong>penser</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "penser", which means "to think". In this sentence, it follows "vais" to create the near future tense, translating to "I am going to think".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a French preposition that can mean "to", "at", or "in" depending on the context. Here, it is part of the expression "penser à quelqu’un" which means "to think of someone".</div><div><strong>toi</strong> This is the strong form (stressed pronoun) for the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s used for emphasis or when there is no verb directly associated with it.</div><div><strong>pendant</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "during". It indicates the duration of time when the action will take place.</div><div><strong>mes</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the first-person singular, meaning "my" in English. It indicates that the following noun belongs to the speaker.</div><div><strong>vacances</strong> This is a French noun meaning "vacation" or "holidays". It is in the plural form to mean "vacations" or "a holiday period".</div>','<h2>Tu marchais avec ton chien tous les jours.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>marchais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "marcher", which means "to walk". It indicates a past habit or a repeated action in the past.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It describes the company or accompaniment in this context.</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "your" in English. It indicates possession and agrees in gender and number with the noun it precedes, which is "chien" in this case, hence it’s masculine singular form.</div><div><strong>chien</strong> This word means "dog" in English. It is a masculine singular noun in French.</div><div><strong>tous les jours</strong> This phrase means "every day" in English. "Tous" translates to "every" and "jours" translates to "days", indicating the repetition of the action.</div>','<h2>Évite d’arriver en retard pour ton rendez-vous.</h2> <div><strong>Évite</strong> This comes from the French verb "éviter", meaning "to avoid". It is in the imperative form, commanding the listener to avoid something. Here, it is in the second-person singular (informal).</div> <div><strong>d’arriver</strong> This is a combination of "de", which can mean "to" or "of", and "arriver", which means "to arrive". When combined as "d’arriver", it often conveys the meaning of "from arriving" or "to arrive" in the infinitive form, which is often used after verbs like "éviter".</div> <div><strong>en retard</strong> This phrase means "late". "En" can mean "in", and "retard" translates to "delay". So, the phrase "en retard" translates directly to "in delay" but is understood as "late".</div> <div><strong>pour ton</strong> "Pour" means "for" and "ton" is the possessive adjective "your" for masculine singular nouns in French. It signifies that something belongs or pertains to the person being addressed.</div> <div><strong>rendez-vous</strong> This is a French noun meaning "appointment" or "meeting". It signifies a scheduled event that one would attend, hence the importance of not being late.</div>','<h2>Tu restais dans ton lit pour boire un café.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is the subject of the sentence and refers to the person being spoken to informally.</div><div><strong>restais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect indicative form of the verb "rester", which means "to stay". It indicates an ongoing past action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "in" or "inside" in English. It indicates location or position.</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the second-person singular in French. It translates to "your" in English and modifies "lit" to indicate possession.</div><div><strong>lit</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "bed" in English. It is the place where one sleeps.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "for" in English. It introduces the purpose or motive for the action.</div><div><strong>boire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb meaning "to drink" in English. It is used here to express the action of drinking.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is an indefinite article in French meaning "a" or "an" in English. It is used here before the noun "café" to indicate a singular, nonspecific item.</div><div><strong>café</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "coffee" in English. It is what the subject intends to drink.</div>','<h2>Je vois ton père, mais le mien n’est pas encore ici.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating the person who is performing the action.</div><div><strong>vois</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "voir", which means "to see". It details the action the subject is performing.</div><div><strong>ton</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English. It modifies the noun that follows it to show ownership by the second-person singular.</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a noun meaning "father" in English. It is the direct object in this part of the sentence, receiving the action of the verb "vois".</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This is a conjunction translating to "but" in English. It introduces a contrast or opposition.</div><div><strong>le mien</strong> This is a possessive pronoun in French, equivalent to "mine" in English. It replaces a noun previously mentioned, in this case, referring to the speaker’s father.</div><div><strong>n’est</strong> This contraction consists of "ne", a negation particle, and "est", which is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "être" (to be). Together, "n’est" translates to "is not" in English, within a negative construction.</div><div><strong>pas encore</strong> The phrase combines "pas", which is typically the second part of a negation in French, and "encore", meaning "yet" in English. Together, they indicate that the action has not happened thus far.</div><div><strong>ici</strong> This is an adverb meaning "here" in English. It denotes location and completes the idea that the subject’s father is not yet present in this location.</div>','<h2>Tu as vérifié l’huile dans ta voiture.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>as</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the past tense (passé composé).</div><div><strong>vérifié</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "vérifier", which means "to check". It is used here in combination with the auxiliary verb "as" to express a completed action.</div><div><strong>l’huile</strong> This is the noun "huile" with the definite article "l’" before it, meaning "the oil". In French, the article "l’" is used because the noun begins with a vowel sound, making it easier to pronounce.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "in" or "inside".</div><div><strong>ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective "ton" in its form before a feminine noun starting with a vowel, meaning "your" in English. It indicates possession or belonging to the second-person singular (informally).</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "car". It’s feminine, which is why the possessive adjective "ta" is used.</div>','<h2>Je veux savoir comment s’est passée ta journée.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This verb comes from "vouloir", which means "to want". Here, it is in the first-person singular present tense and means "I want".</div><div><strong>savoir</strong> From the verb "savoir", meaning "to know", this is the infinitive form. It’s used after "veux" to indicate the desire to know something.</div><div><strong>comment</strong> This word means "how" in English. It’s an adverb used here to start the question about the day.</div><div><strong>s’est passée</strong> This is a compound past tense construction (passé composé) of the verb "se passer", meaning "to happen" or "to occur". It denotes how something has happened, with "s’est" being a part of the auxiliary "être" in the third-person singular, and "passée" being the past participle of "se passer", agreeing in gender and number with the day (assumed feminine singular "la journée").</div><div><strong>ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective "your" in the singular informal form, modifying "journée" to indicate possession.</div><div><strong>journée</strong> This noun means "day", referring to the course of the day. It is feminine and singular, which in this sentence is the object of the speaker’s inquiry.</div>','<h2>Ta voiture est moins rapide que la mienne.</h2><div><strong>Ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French for "your" associated with feminine singular nouns. It indicates that the car mentioned is owned by the person being spoken to.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine noun in French that means "car". It is the subject of the sentence, owned by the person addressed (your car).</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "ta voiture" with the information that follows.</div><div><strong>moins</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "less". It is used in comparative structures to indicate that something has a lower degree or amount of a certain quality.</div><div><strong>rapide</strong> This is an adjective that means "fast" or "quick" in English. In this comparative phrase, it qualifies the speed of the car.</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a conjunction that means "than" in English. It is used to introduce the second element in a comparison. </div><div><strong>la mienne</strong> This is a possessive pronoun in French meaning "mine". It replaces a noun, indicating that the subject is being compared with something belonging to the speaker. The use of "la" indicates that it is replacing a feminine singular noun (the car).</div>','<h2>Tu vas toujours avoir besoin de ta mère.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English (informal). It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vas</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go". Here, it’s used to form the near future tense, indicating an action that is going to happen.</div><div><strong>toujours</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "always". It indicates that the action is one that perpetually or constantly occurs.</div><div><strong>avoir</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb that means "to have". In this sentence, it is used after the verb "vas" to create the near future tense, meaning "are going to have".</div><div><strong>besoin de</strong> This phrase translates to "need of" in English. It is used to express necessity or requirement.</div><div><strong>ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person singular, meaning "your" (informal). It modifies the noun "mère" to indicate possession.</div><div><strong>mère</strong> This is a French noun meaning "mother". It’s the object of the sentence, indicating who the subject will always need.</div>','<h2>Y a-t-il un climatiseur dans ta voiture?</h2><div><strong>Y a-t-il</strong> This is a French phrase used to form a question. "Y a" is an informal contraction of "il y a" which means "there is" or "there are". The "-t-il" is a formality that is added to make a yes/no question sound better phonetically when the verb ends in a vowel and is followed by "il". The complete phrase "Y a-t-il" can be translated as "Is there" or "Are there".</div><div><strong>un climatiseur</strong> This phrase means "an air conditioner". "Un" is the masculine indefinite article "a", and "climatiseur" is the noun for "air conditioner".</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This word translates to "in" in English. It is a preposition that indicates location.</div><div><strong>ta</strong> This is the possessive adjective "your" in English, used in front of a feminine or masculine singular noun that is owned by the person being spoken to (informal).</div><div><strong>voiture?</strong> The noun "voiture" means "car" in English. The question mark indicates that this sentence is a question.</div>','<h2>Tu es partie sans tes clés.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it’s used as an auxiliary verb to form the past tense.</div><div><strong>partie</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "partir", which means "to leave". This participle agrees in gender and number with the subject, which here assumes the subject (tu) is female. If the subject were male, it would be "parti".</div><div><strong>sans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "without". It indicates the absence of something.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the second-person singular possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "your" in English. It’s used here to show ownership of the keys.</div><div><strong>clés</strong> This is a French noun meaning "keys". It is plural because the possessive adjective "tes" already implies the plural form. In French, the noun agrees in number with the adjective.</div>','<h2>Le barbier coupera tes cheveux.</h2><div><strong>Le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>barbier</strong> This is a French noun meaning "barber". It is masculine and singular to agree with the preceding article "le".</div><div><strong>coupera</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "couper", which means "to cut". It indicates that the action will take place in the future.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the second-person singular possessive adjective in French, meaning "your". It modifies the noun it precedes to show ownership.</div><div><strong>cheveux</strong> This is a French noun meaning "hair" in plural form. The possessive adjective "tes" refers to this noun, indicating that the hair belongs to the person being spoken to.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que tes parents sont encore ensemble?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This French phrase is used to form questions. It roughly translates to "Is it that" in English, and is placed at the beginning of a statement to turn it into a question.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective for the second-person singular in French, equivalent to "your" in English when speaking about more than one item or person (plural).</div><div><strong>parents</strong> This is a French noun meaning "parents". It is plural and refers to a person’s mother and father.</div><div><strong>sont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject in plural.</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "still" or "yet". It’s used to indicate that a situation continues to be the case.</div><div><strong>ensemble</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "together". It indicates that two or more people or things are in a state of togetherness or union.</div>','<h2>Tu seras payé selon tes compétences.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>seras</strong> This is the future tense of the verb "être" in the second-person singular, which means "will be". This conveys a future action or state concerning the subject.</div><div><strong>payé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "payer", which means "to pay". When combined with "seras", it forms the future passive voice, meaning "you will be paid".</div><div><strong>selon</strong> This is a preposition meaning "according to" in English. It introduces the standard or criteria by which something will be done.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person singular, meaning "your". It indicates ownership or possession in relation to the subject "tu".</div><div><strong>compétences</strong> This is the noun meaning "skills" or "competencies" in English. It is the object of the preposition "selon" and is what the payment is based on.</div>','<h2>Tu lisais tes messages sur Internet.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lisais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "lire", which means "to read". This tense is used to describe an ongoing or habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective in the second-person singular in French. It indicates ownership and is equivalent to "your" in English. It agrees in number and gender with the noun it modifies, which, in this case, is "messages" (plural).</div><div><strong>messages</strong> This is a French noun meaning "messages". It is in the plural form to match the possessive adjective "tes".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition that can be translated to "on" in English. It is used here to indicate the medium through which the messages are being read.</div><div><strong>Internet</strong> This is a French noun meaning "Internet". While a loan word from English, it is used in the same way to refer to the global system of interconnected computer networks.</div>','<h2>Tu payais trop cher pour tes pistaches.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when addressing one person in an informal context. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>payais</strong> This is the second-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "payer", which means "to pay". The imperfect tense is used to express a habit or a repeated action in the past.</div><div><strong>trop cher</strong> This is a phrase meaning "too expensive". "Trop" translates to "too" and "cher" translates to "expensive". Together, they describe the cost being higher than what is considered reasonable.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition meaning "for". It indicates the purpose of the payment in this context.</div><div><strong>tes</strong> This is the possessive adjective "your" in the second-person singular, used with plural nouns. It indicates that the pistachios belong to the person being addressed.</div><div><strong>pistaches</strong> This is the French word for "pistachios". It is a plural noun referring to the snacks for which the payment is being made.</div>','<h2>Chaque élève a son propre ordinateur.</h2><div><strong>Chaque</strong> This is the French word for "each" or "every". It is used to indicate that each individual member of a group is included.</div><div><strong>élève</strong> This is the French word for "student". In this context, it serves as the subject of the sentence. It is a singular noun that could refer to a student of any gender.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". Here, it connects the subject (each student) to what they possess (their own computer).</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his", "her", or "its" in French. Here, it’s used to express possession and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies (ordinateur), not with the subject.</div><div><strong>propre</strong> This is an adjective that means "own" in English, indicating personal possession or something that specifically belongs to someone.</div><div><strong>ordinateur</strong> This is the French word for "computer". It’s the direct object of the sentence, indicating what each student has.</div>','<h2>Et vous êtes tombé sur son répondeur?</h2><div><strong>Et</strong> This word translates to "and" in English. It’s a conjunction often used to connect phrases or clauses.</div><div><strong>vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. When used with "êtes", it is the formal or plural form of address.</div><div><strong>êtes</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it is paired with "vous" to form a polite or plural statement or question.</div><div><strong>tombé</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "tomber", which means "to fall". In this context, it means "ended up", as in "ended up on" someone’s voicemail, and agrees with the gender of the speaker if it were a woman it would be "tombée".</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a French preposition that can mean "on", "upon", or "over". Here it is used to indicate that someone has reached or encountered someone’s voicemail when attempting to call.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his, her, its". The gender and number match the noun it modifies, which is "répondeur" in this case (masculine, singular).</div><div><strong>répondeur</strong> This is a common noun in French which means "answering machine" or "voicemail". Here, it is the object of the sentence.</div>','<h2>Il a eu une voiture jouet pour son anniversaire.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>a eu</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "avoir" in the compound past tense (passé composé), which means "to have". Here, it signifies that he received or got something in the past.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, used before feminine singular nouns and is equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. It indicates that we’re talking about a single item, which is not specifically known to the listener.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French that means "car".</div><div><strong>jouet</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "toy" in English. In this context, it’s used as an adjective and takes the feminine form to agree with "voiture". It modifies the noun "voiture" to specify that it is a toy car.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It’s used to indicate the reason or purpose of something.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective, which in this context means "his". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "anniversaire".</div><div><strong>anniversaire</strong> This is a masculine singular noun that means "birthday" in English.</div>','<h2>Il aide son père à faire la vaisselle.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>aide</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French which means "his" or "her" in English. Here, it is used to show that the father belongs to the subject (he is his father).</div><div><strong>père</strong> This is a French noun meaning "father". It is the direct object of the verb "help".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition in French that is used in this context to introduce an infinitive verb, often translated to "to" in English.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is an infinitive verb in French which means "to do" or "to make". In this sentence, it is part of the infinitive phrase "à faire la vaisselle", which together means "to do the dishes".</div><div><strong>la vaisselle</strong> This phrase means "the dishes" in English. It is the object of the phrase "à faire" indicating what is being done.</div>','<h2>Il sait très bien chanter pour son âge.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the French third-person singular pronoun, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to a male.</div><div><strong>sait</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "savoir", which means "to know". In this context, it means that he has the knowledge or skill.</div><div><strong>très bien</strong> This phrase translates to "very well" in English. It is an adverbial phrase that qualifies how well the subject can perform the action of the verb.</div><div><strong>chanter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to sing" in English. It is the action that the subject knows how to do very well.</div><div><strong>pour son âge</strong> This phrase translates to "for his age" in English. It implies that his ability to sing is impressive or notable given his age. "son" is a possessive adjective meaning "his", and "âge" is a noun meaning "age".</div>','<h2>Il a quelque chose à demander à son professeur.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used as an auxiliary verb.</div><div><strong>quelque chose</strong> This phrase means "something" in English. It is the direct object of the verb "have" and refers to an unspecified thing that he wants to ask for.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This preposition means "to" in English and is often used to indicate direction, purpose, or recipient of an action.</div><div><strong>demander</strong> This is the infinitive form of a verb meaning "to ask" in English. Here, it’s used after "à" to create the phrase "to ask for".</div><div><strong>son</strong> This possessive adjective means "his" in English, showing ownership or relationship to the subject.</div><div><strong>professeur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "teacher" or "professor" in English. In this sentence, it’s the person to whom the subject wants to ask something.</div>','<h2>Il met son casque, puisqu’il pourrait tomber.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>met</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "mettre", which means "to put" or "to place". In this sentence, it indicates the action of the subject.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, meaning "his" or "her" in English. It is used to indicate ownership or relation to the subject, and agrees with the gender and number of the noun it precedes, in this case, "casque" (helmet).</div><div><strong>casque</strong> This is a French noun meaning "helmet". It is the object of the sentence, the item being put on by the subject.</div><div><strong>puisqu’il</strong> This is a conjunction phrase made up of "puisque" which means "since" or "because", and "il" which again means "he". In this context, it introduces a reason or an explanation for the action described in the first part of the sentence.</div><div><strong>pourrait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to". In this sentence, it expresses a possibility or potentiality.</div><div><strong>tomber</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to fall" in English. It is used here together with "pourrait" to indicate what might happen, providing the reason behind wearing the helmet.</div>','<h2>Il finirait son travail, mais il est trop fatigué.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of both clauses in the sentence.</div><div><strong>finirait</strong> This is a verb in the conditional tense, the conditional form of "finir" which means "to finish". It indicates an action that would happen under certain conditions.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French meaning "his". It indicates that something belongs to the subject, in this case, the "travail" (work).</div><div><strong>travail</strong> This is a French noun meaning "work". It’s the object of the verb "finirait", referring to the work he would finish.</div><div><strong>mais</strong> This is a French conjunction meaning "but". It introduces a contrast or exception to the statement made earlier.</div><div><strong>il est</strong> "il" again stands for "he" and "est" is the third-person singular present tense of the verb "être", meaning "is". Together, they indicate the state of being of the subject "he".</div><div><strong>trop</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "too". It’s used to modify the adjective "fatigué" to indicate an excessive degree.</div><div><strong>fatigué</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "tired". When combined with "trop", it indicates that the subject is too tired to do something, in this case, to finish his work.</div>','<h2>Il loue souvent un kayak pour s’amuser avec son fils.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It serves as the subject of the sentence, referring to an unspecified male individual.</div><div><strong>loue</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "louer", which means "to rent" or "to hire". It describes the action that the subject is performing.</div><div><strong>souvent</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "often". It modifies the verb and indicates the frequency of the action.</div><div><strong>un kayak</strong> This phrase means "a kayak" in English. "Un" is the indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, and "kayak" is a masculine noun borrowed from the Inuit language, used in French to refer to the small narrow boat.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "for". It is used here to indicate the purpose of the action.</div><div><strong>s’amuser</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "amuser", which means "to have fun" or "to enjoy oneself". The reflexive pronoun "s’" indicates that the action is performed by the subject upon himself.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It introduces the idea of accompaniment.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the subject.</div><div><strong>fils</strong> This word translates to "son" in English. It is a masculine singular noun, referring to the male offspring of the subject.</div>','<h2>Il dormait à son bureau, car il travaillait trop.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>dormait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "dormir", which means "to sleep". It indicates a past continuous action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition in French that can mean "to", "at", or "in" among other things. In this sentence, it’s used to indicate the location where "il" was sleeping.</div><div><strong>son</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, and it means "his". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "bureau" in this case.</div><div><strong>bureau,</strong> This is a French noun meaning "desk" or "office". It’s masculine and singular here, which is why the possessive adjective "son" is used.</div><div><strong>car</strong> This is a conjunction in French meaning "because" or "for". It introduces the reason or cause for the previous statement.</div><div><strong>il</strong> Again, this is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s used for the second time in this sentence as the subject.</div><div><strong>travaillait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "travailler", which means "to work". Like "dormait", it indicates a past continuous action or a habitual action in the past.</div><div><strong>trop.</strong> This is an adverb meaning "too much" in English. It describes the extent to which "il" was working, which ultimately justifies why he was sleeping at his desk.</div>','<h2>Elle devrait nettoyer sa chambre.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devrait</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the conditional tense of the verb "devoir", which means "must" or "should". It suggests an obligation or a recommendation.</div><div><strong>nettoyer</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to clean" in English. In the sentence, it serves as the main action that "she" should carry out.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "her" in English. It indicates that the noun it modifies belongs to the feminine subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>chambre</strong> This means "room" or "bedroom" in English. In the sentence, it’s the object that she should clean and is owned by the subject "elle".</div>','<h2>Elle aide sa fille à s’habiller.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a female person.</div><div><strong>aide</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". In this sentence, it describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "her" in English. It indicates that the following noun "fille" (daughter) belongs to the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fille</strong> This is a French noun meaning "daughter". It’s the direct object receiving the action of the verb "aide".</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition that is often used to indicate purpose or direction. In this case, it is used before the infinitive "s’habiller" to describe the action being helped with.</div><div><strong>s’habiller</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "habiller", which means "to dress". The reflexive pronoun "s’" indicates that the action is being performed by the subject onto herself, meaning "to dress oneself". In this sentence, it refers to the daughter dressing herself with help from "Elle".</div>','<h2>Elle doit payer avec sa carte de crédit.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>doit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". It indicates an obligation on the subject to perform the action that follows.</div><div><strong>payer</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "payer", which means "to pay". In this sentence, it is the action that the subject must do.</div><div><strong>avec</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "with". It is used here to indicate the means by which the payment will be made.</div><div><strong>sa</strong> This is a possessive adjective in the third-person singular feminine form, meaning "her". It indicates that the credit card belongs to the subject, "elle".</div><div><strong>carte de crédit</strong> This is a noun phrase meaning "credit card". It is the object that is being used to pay.</div>','<h2>Elle a fait du ski deux fois dans sa vie.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a female individual.</div><div><strong>a fait</strong> This is a form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make" in English. Here, it’s conjugated in the third-person singular past tense (passé composé), meaning "has done" or "did". "A fait" is used to describe an action that was completed in the past.</div><div><strong>du ski</strong> This is a phrase meaning "skiing". "Du" is a partitive article used in French before masculine nouns to indicate an unspecified quantity, similar to "some" in English. Here, it is used with "ski", which means the same in English, to talk about the activity of skiing in general.</div><div><strong>deux fois</strong> This phrase means "twice", indicating the number of times the action was performed. "Deux" means "two" and "fois" means "times" in English.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in" when referring to an unspecified or general time, and "inside" when referring to a specific place.</div><div><strong>sa vie</strong> This phrase means "her life". "Sa" is a possessive adjective meaning "her", used here to show ownership or relation to the subject "elle". "Vie" is the French word for "life".</div>','<h2>Elle fermera la porte de sa chambre d’hôtel.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fermera</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "fermer", which means "to close". It indicates a future action that she will perform.</div><div><strong>la porte</strong> This translates to "the door" in English. "La" is the feminine singular definite article "the", and "porte" is the noun "door".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in French. In this context, it means "of" or "from", indicating possession or belonging.</div><div><strong>sa chambre</strong> This phrase translates to "her room" in English. "Sa" is the possessive adjective "her", and "chambre" is the noun "room". The possessive adjective agrees in gender and number with the thing possessed, not with the possessor.</div><div><strong>d’hôtel</strong> This means "hotel" in English and is used here in the form of a noun modifier to specify the type of room mentioned. "D’" is the contraction of "de" (of) and "hôtel" caused by the following word starting with a vowel sound.</div>','<h2>Elle est arrivée de l’école et ensuite elle a joué avec sa soeur.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est arrivée</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine form of the past participle of the verb "arriver", which means "to arrive". It is used with "être" as an auxiliary to form the passé composé tense, indicating a completed action.</div><div><strong>de l’école</strong> This phrase means "from school". <em>de</em> is a preposition indicating origin, and <em>l’école</em> (with <em>l’</em> being the elided form of <em>la</em> due to the following vowel sound) is a feminine singular noun meaning "the school".</div><div><strong>et</strong> This is the French word for "and". It’s a conjunction that connects two independent clauses in this sentence.</div><div><strong>ensuite</strong> This is an adverb meaning "then" or "afterwards". It is used to describe the sequence of events.</div><div><strong>elle</strong> This word is repeated as the subject pronoun for the second independent clause, also referring to the female person mentioned earlier.</div><div><strong>a joué</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the passé composé tense of the verb "jouer", which means "to play". It is composed of the auxiliary verb "avoir" in the present tense (<em>a</em>) and the past participle of "jouer" (<em>joué</em>).</div><div><strong>avec sa soeur</strong> This phrase means "with her sister". <em>avec</em> is a preposition meaning "with", <em>sa</em> is a possessive adjective meaning "her" that agrees in gender and number with <em>soeur</em>, a feminine singular noun meaning "sister".</div>','<h2>Il a fait ses premiers pas aujourd’hui.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a fait</strong> This is a conjugated form of the verb "faire", which means "to do" or "to make" in English. "A fait" is the third-person singular past tense (passé composé) and translates to "has done" or "made".</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "his" in English. It indicates ownership of the following noun by the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>premiers</strong> This is the masculine plural form of the ordinal number "premier", which means "first" in English. It’s used here to describe "pas" (steps), indicating that they are the initial ones.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word has several meanings in French. In this context, it means "steps" and refers to the action of walking. "Ses premiers pas" translates to "his first steps".</div><div><strong>aujourd’hui</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "today". It’s used here to specify when the action of taking the first steps occurred.</div>','<h2>Il a aidé ses petites soeurs à monter la colline.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>a aidé</strong> This is the past tense form of the verb "aider", which means "to help". The verb is in the passé composé tense, which is used to express actions that have been completed in the past.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, equivalent to "his" or "her" in English. In this case, it’s used to indicate that the "petites soeurs" (little sisters) belong to the subject "he".</div><div><strong>petites soeurs</strong> This phrase translates to "little sisters" in English. "Petites" is the feminine plural form of "petit", which means "little", and "soeurs" is the plural of "soeur", which means "sister". The adjective "petites" agrees with the feminine plural noun "soeurs".</div><div><strong>à monter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "monter", preceded by the preposition "à". In English, this translates to "to climb". In French, the preposition "à" is often used before an infinitive to indicate purpose or intention.</div><div><strong>la colline</strong> This phrase means "the hill" in English. "La" is the feminine singular article "the", and "colline" is the feminine noun "hill".</div>','<h2>Il attachait ses lacets avant le match de tennis.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>attachait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "attacher", which means "to tie". This tense is used to describe a past, ongoing action or habit.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective in French, meaning "his" or "her" in English. It indicates that the "laces" belong to the subject ("he").</div><div><strong>lacets</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "lacet", which means "lace" or "laces" in English. It’s the object of the sentence that he is tying.</div><div><strong>avant</strong> This is a preposition meaning "before" in English. It’s used to indicate a time relationship between tying the laces and the tennis match.</div><div><strong>le</strong> This is the definite article in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "match" to denote a specific tennis match.</div><div><strong>match de tennis</strong> This is a noun phrase meaning "tennis match" in English. "Match" is borrowed from English and "tennis" is the same in both languages. Together, they denote the event taking place.</div>','<h2>Il teste ses nouveaux skis sur une montagne abrupte.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>teste</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "tester", which means "to test" or "to try out". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>ses</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his" or "her" in English. It shows ownership of the following noun by the subject.</div><div><strong>nouveaux</strong> This is the masculine plural form of the adjective "nouveau", meaning "new". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "skis".</div><div><strong>skis</strong> This is a noun borrowed from English, meaning the same as in English. It is the direct object of the verb "teste", representing what is being tested.</div><div><strong>sur</strong> This is a preposition meaning "on" or "upon" in English. It introduces the location where the action is taking place.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is an indefinite article meaning "a" or "an" in English. It precedes the feminine singular noun to which it refers.</div><div><strong>montagne</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "mountain". It is the location in the prepositional phrase started by "sur".</div><div><strong>abrupte</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "abrupt", meaning "steep" or "precipitous". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, "montagne", and describes the characteristic of the mountain.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);